{
  "concept": "In a connected weighted undirected graph, a spanning tree is an acyclic subset of edges that touches every vertex, and a minimum spanning tree minimizes the total weight — but it need not be unique.",
  "figureType": "network_diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Nine circular vertices with light-gray fill, dark outline, italic serif labels: a (left, middle height), b (upper left), c (top center), d (upper right), e (right, middle height), f (lower right), g (bottom center), h (bottom left), i (center)",
    "Fourteen straight undirected edges: a-b, b-c, c-d, d-e, a-h, b-h, h-i, h-g, g-i, i-c, c-f, g-f, d-f, e-f",
    "Integer weight labels placed beside each edge: a-b=4, b-c=8, c-d=7, d-e=9, a-h=8, b-h=11, h-i=7, h-g=1, g-i=6, i-c=2, c-f=4, g-f=2, d-f=14, e-f=10",
    "Broad gray shading band drawn behind the eight tree edges (default tree: a-b, b-c, c-d, d-e, c-i, c-f, h-g, g-f)",
    "Thin black strokes for the six non-tree edges (a-h, b-h, h-i, g-i, d-f, e-f)",
    "Running-total readout panel showing accumulated weight of shaded edges",
    "Edge-inspection info box showing the clicked edge's endpoints, weight, and in-tree / not-in-tree status",
    "Vertex 'reached' marker (filled accent ring) used during the step-through build",
    "Legend: shaded band = tree edge, thin line = excluded edge"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "inspectEdge",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Each edge is a candidate connection with a cost; selecting one shows its weight and whether including it would create a cycle or is part of the chosen spanning tree."
    },
    {
      "id": "treeVariant",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Which minimum spanning tree",
      "options": ["Tree A (uses b–c)", "Tree B (uses a–h)"],
      "default": "Tree A (uses b–c)"
    },
    {
      "id": "buildTree",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Add next tree edge",
      "states": ["0 edges", "1 edge", "2 edges", "3 edges", "4 edges", "5 edges", "6 edges", "7 edges", "8 edges — spanning"],
      "default": "0 edges"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The wiring problem",
      "narration": "Nine pins must be connected; every possible interconnection is an edge and its weight is the length of wire it would need. All fourteen candidate connections are shown, none chosen yet.",
      "state": { "buildTree": "0 edges", "treeVariant": "Tree A (uses b–c)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Cheapest links first",
      "narration": "Adding edges one at a time, the light connections h–g (1), g–f (2) and c–i (2) appear. Watch the reached-vertex markers spread outward — the set stays acyclic the whole time.",
      "state": { "buildTree": "3 edges", "treeVariant": "Tree A (uses b–c)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Spanning every vertex",
      "narration": "By the eighth edge all nine vertices are reached with exactly 8 = 9 − 1 edges and no cycle. The running total is 37 — the minimum possible wire length.",
      "state": { "buildTree": "8 edges — spanning", "treeVariant": "Tree A (uses b–c)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare the edges left out",
      "narration": "Click the heavy unshaded edges such as d–f (14) and b–h (11): each would close a cycle and cost more than an edge already in the tree, so it is excluded.",
      "state": { "buildTree": "8 edges — spanning", "treeVariant": "Tree A (uses b–c)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "A minimum spanning tree is not unique",
      "narration": "Swap b–c (8) for a–h (8): the gray band shifts, the tree still spans all nine vertices with no cycle, and the total weight is still 37.",
      "state": { "buildTree": "8 edges — spanning", "treeVariant": "Tree B (uses a–h)" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A minimum spanning tree is defined by its total weight, not by its shape — swapping b–c for the equally heavy a–h gives a different tree that is just as minimal at 37.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape layout, roughly the classic CLRS arrangement: a at far left mid-height; b upper-left; c top-center; d upper-right; e far right mid-height; f lower-right; g bottom-center; h bottom-left; i center, slightly above g. Vertices are light-gray filled circles with black outlines and italic serif letters. Edges are straight black lines; tree edges get a thick pale-gray band (about four times the line width) drawn behind the black stroke so the black line still shows. Weight numerals in large serif type sit just off each edge, outside the polygon where possible (8 above b–c, 7 above c–d, 4 left of a–b, 9 upper-right of d–e, 11 beside b–h, 8 lower-left of a–h, 7 beside h–i, 6 beside g–i, 2 right of i–c, 4 right of c–f, 14 beside d–f, 10 lower-right of e–f, 1 below h–g, 2 below g–f). Use a single accent color (e.g. orange) only for interactive feedback: a clicked edge and its weight label, and reached-vertex rings during the step-through. Keep a small total-weight readout and a status line for the selected edge in a corner, visually separate from the graph."
}

